  {
    "episode_id": 74126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, in some ways, Harvard and Oxford are maybe more closely related than most, right? Harvard is well over 350 years old. So there is that sense of being somewhat ancient for the New World. And of course, Harvard was founded by a graduate of Cambridge University. So there is probably more resonance there than there are with an awful lot of American University. But of course everything is different. For one thing, the American system of education focuses around lectures, particularly in the early years of university. You can find yourself sitting in a lecture hall with 300 of your fellow students. It's also particularly against Oxford. You know, Harvard is one of the finest institutions in the United States. A much more significant percentage of American 18-year-olds are in university than is true in Great Britain. And so in the UK, when you go to university, you're going to sort of maybe elite's not quite the right word, but you're going to in some ways a less diverse and in other ways much better educated group of people then you would be going to in an American university where something like, you know, almost half of the population goes on to some form of university education.",
