    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think like so many Americans who arrive in Oxford, I had no idea what I was getting into. And it is enormously daunting, even for those of us who had pretty elite educations in the United States, and it's enormously daunting. I mean, everything from the dining halls and high table to the gown, it's all profoundly foreign to somebody who who went to the United States and American University. One of the wonderful things about the Hall, of course, was that it is, I think, one of the more welcoming of the colleges because of its emphasis on sport. You know, an American who arrives at Oxford, what's the way I can put this sort of diplomatically? It's easier to get to know people in the United States than it is in Great Britain. And so for me, anyway, I was a rower. For me, having that link to my British fellow students was an enormously valuable way to get to know people. I sort of found, I hate to trade in cultural stereotypes, but I sort of found in the United States, as natural as can be, to go up to somebody in the dining hall and introduce yourself and say hello in Great Britain. And there's a little bit more of, you know, you need a reason. You need a",
