    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "So, no, there weren't. There was a South African who was there at the same time. You know, it was a relatively small college. I think there's fewer at fewer Rhodes scholars at Teddy Hall than there might be at Christchurch or at New. And it's sort of a wonderful surprise. You know, I mean, most Americans wouldn't know much about the college system or about the colleges, but if they do, they've probably heard of the very high-profile ones they've heard of Christchurch, maybe. So Teddy Hall is sort of an unknown and you know then you visit it and you see that it's actually this very intimate environment with particularly friendly people, some of the oldest architecture of course in Oxford University and it kind of catches your your imagination. The Rhodes scholarship of course offers you I guess there may be, I don't know, 90 or so road scholars in your peer group at any given time in Oxford. So it does offer a natural peer group, folks that you can hang around with and share stories of this exotic new land that you find yourself in",
