    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and even the academic course was a little bit of a challenge. I mean, of course, you know, if someone, not from the UK knows one thing about Oxford, it's probably the PPE concentration, the major And so I sort of started out with the idea of maybe doing PPE. And as it turned out, I'd done a program very similar to PPE as an undergraduate, so I had conversations. It's all very informal at Oxford, which again is a little bit alien to our way of doing things. But ultimately, after a number of conversations, I chose to have that MPhil. And I guess the thing that I found most different and surprising in many ways wonderful was the sort of informal quality of the education. Again, in the United States, you're sitting in a lecture hall and you have set exams. You know, it's all very regimented. You know, I remember in my first couple of months thinking, okay, apparently I'm getting a Masters, you know, an M fill in Latin American studies. But wait, where are the classes? What exactly am I",
