    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "I'll take you to one of, as we say over here, one of the PG stories. Obviously, just a wonderful and traditional thing. Again, these iconic images of Oxford rowing, it's a spectacular feeling. It's also, by the way, one of the challenges, of course, of anybody who spends time in England generally or at Oxford is you can easily get cooped up in your flat or in the when it's pouring rain and chilly outside. So the opportunity to get some exercise and be out doing sports was wonderful. But of course, you know, there's such a tradition of both rugby and rowing at the hall that you sort of feel you're participating in something that is really important to the place. No, and it was just magnificent. I still am close friends with a number of the people that I rode with at Teddy Hall, even though that was what some 20, 30 years ago now. Yeah, the story I can tell you, this is a classic, classic Oxford story. There are some that I probably shouldn't tell, but I was elected captain of boats of Teddy Hall and you know, a very different system than we have over here. So I'm elected captain of boats and we have this fancy dinner, black tie, dinner jackets at the Randolph Hotel, you know, and we're all needless to say, drinking quite a bit of wine and other things. And it turns out, and I didn't know this until I was standing in the hallway of the Randolph Hotel afterwards, it turns out that there's a tradition that the rugby players who are also at the rowing dinner strip the new captain of boat completely naked, wrap him or her. I never actually thought about what happened if it were her, but wrap him in the college flag, march you down the street and throw you in the college well. So yes, I had the experience of being stripped in a matter of two or three seconds entirely of my clothing in the lobby of the Randolph Hotel, carried down Broad Street and thrown into the college well. That is one of those experiences that I won't soon forget.",
