    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Well, probably more than anything else, a lack of creative thinking on my part. I think it may still be true, but an awful lot of wayward, somewhat accomplished graduates find themselves either in the finance world or in the consulting world. So it was probably as a lack of creativity as much as anything else. But actually, it was a bit more intentional than that. I had it in the back of my mind in university that I would probably at some point go into public service. And I'm not sure I would have known that it would be an into elected office, but I knew that I wanted to go to work for the government at some point. However, I thought it's probably not a good idea to start there. Probably a good idea to develop some marketable skills. Turns out that's the case because people do in fact lose elections from time to time. I really did want to get some experience in business prior to at some point getting into government service. It was one of those things where I just thought, wow, I can learn an awful lot. In fact, that turned out to be true. being part of Wall Street is not necessarily a political asset in the United States, but an awful lot of what I learned at the bank was actually enormously valuable, subsequently, in",
