  {
    "episode_id": 74126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "I think the key is finding the nexus. I sit on the intelligence committee. That occupies a lot of my time. It's a really interesting committee and as much as we are civilians, we're not in the chain of command of the CIA or the other intelligence agencies. We are civilians who are charged with, first of all, knowing and understanding everything that our intelligence community does, including some pretty dicey activities, and of course providing some oversight to that. So the key really is when you're talking about something very technical, like what should be the limitations on the ability of our intelligence operation to capture emails abroad? Our intelligence operations don't capture emails here in the United States, but as you might imagine, they are interested in the emails that can be captured outside of the United States. What happens when an American's email is caught up in that capture? Again, the touchstone has to be, well, what would the good people of Connecticut's Fourth District think about that and always go back to that as opposed to what Jim Himes may think about that?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
