  {
    "episode_id": 74126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "Well, so it was even more heightened than that in the sense that we were actually doing something unprecedented, which is having a very real debate over the results of the presidential election. You know, under our Constitution, the Congress must essentially certify what the Electoral College has determined based on the way people have voted. And yes, we have one of the most bizarre ways of electing the president of any country out there. But the point is that never in our history, well, that's not quite true, once or twice in our history, but have the results of a presidential election been contested on the floor of the House of Representatives in the United States Congress. And of course, in this case, it was an utterly specious point. that the Republicans and the president were making. I mean, our country has been badly, badly damaged by what we are now calling the big lie, this idea that the election was stolen by Joe Biden. And this wasn't an election that was close, right? I mean, and again, we don't need to get into the peculiarities of how America elects its president, but Joe Biden wiped the floor with his opponent, more than 8 million votes in excess of what Donald Trump received. And yet here we are having this unprecedented argument over whether we should accept the results of the election. And when that happens, of course, we knew these people were in Washington. In fact, I'd gone to see the protest the night before just to get a feel for what these folks thought. And I mean, it was people, it's something to think about. My profession requires me to be understanding and to reach out to those people that disagree with me. But I mean, these were people who were suffering under just a bizarre delusion. I mean, We don't have time to get into all the peculiarities, but the election had been stolen, the facts were clear, even though there's not a single fact out there to suggest that that was the case. The movement was saturated with evangelical Christianity. This was done for Jesus, and Jesus is with you on this stuff. It was a truly, truly bizarre moment. And it was bizarre from a security standpoint. I'd always assumed I've been in the capital now for coming on 13 years. I'd always assumed that I was in one of the most secure places on the planet, that if there was an assault on the capital, that as I said to a reporter, a red button gets pressed and all of a sudden everything is locked down and nobody can get in. But of course, the capital was breached and every part of the capital was breached.",
