  {
    "episode_id": 74126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 114,
    "turn_text": "I would absolutely choose what I've been alluding to. I keep using the word intimacy. I know it's not quite the right word. But I would choose that culture, which includes a very welcoming culture of interaction. So back in my day, there were some dawns who had been around for 70 years or something. And they had no reason in the world to talk to me, but they came from a different era. Some of them were veterans of World War II back in the 80s, and they went out of their way at dinners and stuff, and they didn't need to do this to talk to me to tell me their stories. And I think that that's invaluable. I mean, it's one thing to read Milton. It's another to hear the stories of somebody who comes from a radically different time or a radically different place. And again, that's something that maybe on this side of the Atlantic culturally happens more easily than it happens in the UK where the social system is a little more stratified and manners are a little bit more guarded. And so that just that human interaction that I experienced every single day, every single night at the hall was probably the most valuable thing that I got out of the hall.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
